{% extends "layout.njk" %}
{% import "macros.njk" as m %}
{% set title = "Composition" %}
{% set slug = "composition" %}

{% block content %}
<h1>Composition</h1>
<p class="lede">
  The component pages show each piece on its own. This page is about how they
  fit together: which piece owns colour, which owns spacing, which owns text,
  and where the seams between them are. The
  <a href="./patterns-studio.html">studio</a> and
  <a href="./patterns-community.html">community</a> pages put these rules to
  work on real page sections.
</p>

<p>
  <small>
    Examples on this page and the section pages use the class build. Layout that
    the framework leaves to you, such as a grid, is written inline so you can see
    it.
  </small>
</p>

<section id="layers">
  <h2>Who owns what</h2>
  <p>
    Every piece of markup plays one of five roles. Most mistakes come from asking
    one piece to do another's job, such as giving a grid wrapper
    <code>.prose</code>, or a card a margin.
  </p>
  <table class="class-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Role</th><th>Pieces</th><th>Owns</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Layout</td>
        <td><code>.container</code>, <code>.flex</code>, <code>.navbar</code>, CSS grid</td>
        <td>Width, columns, and the gap between siblings. No colour, no text spacing.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Surface</td>
        <td><code>.card</code>, <code>.card-border</code>, <code>.dialog-box</code>, <code>.dropdown-content</code></td>
        <td>A background, a border or shadow, and inner padding. Nothing outside its edge.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Text</td>
        <td><code>.prose</code>, <code>.text-secondary</code>, <code>.link</code>, <code>.badge</code></td>
        <td>Vertical rhythm between headings, paragraphs and lists, and the colour of words.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Control</td>
        <td><code>.label</code>, <code>.input</code>, <code>.btn</code>, <code>.fieldset</code></td>
        <td>Its own box, its own bottom margin, and its states.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Boundary</td>
        <td><code>.retheme</code>, <code>data-color-scheme</code>, <code>.disabled</code>, <code>.error</code></td>
        <td>The colour of everything inside it. Draws nothing itself.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    A section of a page is usually one of each, nested in that order: a layout
    places surfaces, a surface holds text and controls, and a boundary can wrap
    any of them.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="surfaces">
  <h2>Surfaces</h2>
  <p>
    There are three colours to stand on. <code>--bg</code> is the page.
    <code>.card</code> raises a block onto <code>--surface</code> with a shadow.
    <code>.card-border</code> stays on <code>--bg</code> and draws a
    <code>--divider</code> border instead.
  </p>
  <p>
    Surfaces are solid colours, mixed into the page rather than laid over it, so a
    card inside a card is the same colour as its parent and does not darken. To
    separate something inside a card, put it on a <code>.card-border</code>, which
    drops back to the page colour, or split the card with a
    <code>.divider</code> or <code>.card-actions</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Below: text on the page, a service from sparkstonepdx.com on a
    <code>.card</code>, and an item from lzr.life's community kit on a
    <code>.card-border</code>.
  </p>
  {% set classless %}
<div class="prose">
  <p>On the page: I build the systems your business actually runs on.</p>
</div>
<div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(12rem, 1fr)); gap: var(--padding)">
  <article class="card prose">
    <h3>Project rescue</h3>
    <p>take over a build that stalled, or clean up after a vendor</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card-border prose">
    <h3>QR Codes</h3>
    <p class="flex"><a href="#" class="link">PNG</a> <a href="#" class="link">SVG</a></p>
  </article>
</div>
  {% endset %}
  {{ m.demo("comp-surfaces", classless) }}
  <ul>
    <li>Use <code>.card</code> for the thing you want looked at: a service you sell, a call to action.</li>
    <li>Use <code>.card-border</code> for many items of equal weight, such as a grid of downloads, where a shadow on each would be noise.</li>
    <li>A surface sets padding and nothing else outside itself. Space between surfaces comes from the layout around them.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="spacing">
  <h2>Spacing</h2>
  <p>
    Spacing comes from two places, and they do not overlap.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Between siblings in a layout:</strong> the gap. <code>.flex</code>
      has a <code>1rem</code> gap; a grid should use <code>gap: var(--padding)</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Down a column of content:</strong> bottom margins. Form controls,
      labels, alerts and every block inside <code>.prose</code> carry a
      <code>1rem</code> bottom margin, so a stack of them spaces itself with no
      wrapper.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Nothing carries a top margin, so the first child of any box sits flush with
    its padding. The last child keeps its bottom margin, which is why
    <code>.card-actions</code> exists: a footer row gives the end of a card its
    own rule and spacing.
  </p>
  <p>
    The two meet in one place: a <code>.flex .flip</code> row of form fields. Side
    by side, the gap separates them. Stacked on a phone, the gap lands on top of
    each label's own bottom margin and doubles the space. Set
    <code>row-gap: 0</code> on such a row and let the margins do the stacking.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>--padding</code> sizes the inside of many components: badges,
    alerts, menus, the navbar, segmented controls and <code>.card-border</code>.
    Set it on a section to tighten or loosen those insides together. The
    <code>1rem</code> margins between blocks stay as they are.
  </p>
  {% set classless %}
<div class="flex flip">
  <div class="card-border" style="flex: 1">
    <span class="badge">default</span>
    <p class="text-secondary" style="margin-top: 0.5rem">--padding: 1rem</p>
  </div>
  <div class="card-border" style="flex: 1; --padding: 0.5rem">
    <span class="badge">tight</span>
    <p class="text-secondary" style="margin-top: 0.5rem">--padding: 0.5rem on the card</p>
  </div>
</div>
  {% endset %}
  {{ m.demo("comp-spacing", classless) }}
</section>

<section id="text">
  <h2>Text</h2>
  <p>
    The reset removes every margin, and the class build does not style bare
    elements. Headings, paragraphs and lists stack with no space between them
    until something inside <code>.prose</code> spaces them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Put <code>.prose</code> on the box that holds the words: a card, a section's
    text column, an article body. Do not put it on a layout wrapper.
    <code>.prose</code> also spaces <code>section</code>, <code>ul</code> and
    <code>pre</code>, so on a grid it would add margins between your columns.
  </p>
  {% set classless %}
<div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(12rem, 1fr)); gap: var(--padding)">
  <div class="card-border">
    <h3>No prose</h3>
    <p>Heading and paragraph touch.</p>
    <p>So do paragraphs.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="card-border prose">
    <h3>With prose</h3>
    <p>The card spaces its own text.</p>
    <p>The grid around it is untouched.</p>
  </div>
</div>
  {% endset %}
  {{ m.demo("comp-text", classless) }}
  <p>
    Three classes colour words without spacing them. <code>.text-secondary</code>
    is for supporting text: a lede, a date, a caption. <code>.link</code> is for
    words that navigate. <code>.badge</code> is for a short label attached to
    something else, such as a tag or a count.
  </p>
</section>

<section id="actions">
  <h2>Actions</h2>
  <p>
    Buttons come in three weights, and a region should have one filled button.
    <code>.btn</code> is the primary action, <code>.btn-secondary</code> the
    alternative, <code>.btn-ghost</code> anything that should recede, such as a
    menu toggle in a header. Links navigate; buttons do things.
  </p>
  <p>
    When a whole card is the link, make the card the anchor:
    <code>&lt;a class="semantic card"&gt;</code>. <code>.semantic</code> strips the
    link styling and <code>.card</code> comes later in the cascade, so the surface
    wins. Then mark the words that read as the link, here the title, with
    <code>.link</code> so the card still looks clickable. Inside a grid or flex
    row the anchor is laid out as a block, so it needs nothing else.
  </p>
  {% set classless %}
<div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(14rem, 1fr)); gap: var(--padding)">
  <a href="#" class="semantic card prose">
    <h3 class="link">The Map I Never Meant to Build</h3>
    <p>Sixty thousand views, three neighborhoods asking to borrow it, and a little map that got a bit better every spring until it turned into something anyone can run.</p>
  </a>
  <a href="#" class="semantic card prose">
    <h3 class="link">A Library That Fits in a Free Little Library</h3>
    <p>A zine catalog, a printing guide, and an events page, built from a pile of draft copy in about two weeks.</p>
  </a>
</div>
  {% endset %}
  {{ m.demo("comp-card-link", classless) }}
</section>

<section id="forms">
  <h2>Forms</h2>
  <p>
    A <code>.label</code> is the unit of a form. It wraps a <code>&lt;p&gt;</code>
    for the label text, the control, and an optional <code>&lt;small&gt;</code>
    for help or an error, and it spaces all three. Put a checkbox or toggle inside
    a <code>.label</code> and it becomes a row instead. Group related labels in a
    <code>.fieldset</code> with a <code>.fieldset-legend</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <code>&lt;small&gt;</code> is <code>inline-block</code>, so after a field
    narrowed with <code>max-width</code> it sits beside the field instead of under
    it. Give it <code>display: block</code>, as the newsletter form below does.
  </p>
  <p>
    States are generic. <code>aria-invalid="true"</code> or <code>.error</code> on
    a control turns it, and the label text above it, to the error colour.
    Disabling a <code>&lt;fieldset&gt;</code> greys everything inside it, because a
    disabled element is a boundary that sets <code>--chroma: 0</code> for its
    subtree.
  </p>
  <p>
    Below: sparkstonepdx.com's newsletter form with a bad address entered, and
    lzr.life's topic filters.
  </p>
  {% set classless %}
<form class="prose">
  <h2>Newsletter</h2>
  <p>Subscribe to my newsletter to be the first to hear about what I do next</p>
  <label class="label">
    <p>Email</p>
    <input class="input" type="email" value="adam@" aria-invalid="true" style="max-width: 30rem" />
    <small style="display: block">Enter a full email address.</small>
  </label>
  <button class="btn btn-secondary">Subscribe</button>
</form>
<form style="margin-top: var(--padding)">
  <fieldset class="fieldset">
    <legend class="fieldset-legend">Topics</legend>
    <div class="flex" style="flex-wrap: wrap">
      <label class="label"><input type="checkbox" class="checkbox" checked /> single sided</label>
      <label class="label"><input type="checkbox" class="checkbox" /> trifold</label>
      <label class="label"><input type="checkbox" class="checkbox" /> quarters</label>
    </div>
  </fieldset>
</form>
  {% endset %}
  {{ m.demo("comp-forms", classless) }}
</section>

<section id="boundaries">
  <h2>Colour boundaries</h2>
  <p>
    Colour is never set on a component. It is set on a region, and everything in
    the region derives from it. Two attributes draw those regions.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>.retheme</code> with an input such as <code>--primary</code> re-themes
      a subtree: the studio's credit block on a client's site, or an error region.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>data-color-scheme="dark"</code> or <code>"light"</code> flips a
      subtree's scheme and paints its background, so a band can sit dark on a
      light page with no extra classes.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A boundary draws nothing on its own, so put it on the element that already
    exists for layout, such as the section or the card, rather than adding a
    wrapper.
  </p>
  <p>
    Below: a section of lzr.life, in a red that stands in for its own colour,
    with the studio's credit block inside it in the studio's. Boundaries nest,
    and each one reaches only its own subtree.
  </p>
  {% set classless %}
<section class="retheme" style="--primary: #a33b2b; display: grid; gap: var(--padding)">
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>Little Zine Revolution</h3>
    <p>Little actions have ripple effects.</p>
    <a href="#" role="button" class="btn">Explore the Zine Catalog</a>
  </div>
  <aside class="card-border retheme prose" style="--primary: rebeccapurple">
    <h3>Want a site like this?</h3>
    <p>We build accessible, performant sites your users will love.</p>
    <a href="#" role="button" class="btn">Book a free call</a>
  </aside>
</section>
  {% endset %}
  {{ m.demo("comp-boundaries", classless) }}
</section>

<section id="layout">
  <h2>Layout</h2>
  <p>
    The framework gives you a page width, a row, and a bar. Everything else is
    CSS grid with <code>var(--padding)</code> as the gap.
  </p>
  <table class="class-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Need</th><th>Use</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>A page column</td><td><code>.container</code>, once per page, around the main content</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A full-width band with padded content</td><td><code>.container-fluid</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>A row that stacks on phones</td><td><code>.flex .flip</code>; add <code>.reverse</code> to put the second item first</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A header</td><td><code>.navbar</code> with <code>.navbar-start</code> and <code>.navbar-end</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>A grid of cards</td><td><code>grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(14rem, 1fr))</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    See <a href="./layout.html">Layout</a> for the breakpoints, and the
    <a href="./patterns-studio.html">studio</a> and
    <a href="./patterns-community.html">community</a> pages for whole sections
    built from these rules.
  </p>
</section>
{% endblock %}
